{"tool_name":"cnapy","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"cnapy","home":"https://github.com/cnapy-org/CNApy","license":"Apache-2.0","summary":"An integrated environment for metabolic network analysis.","addition_date":"2022-06-06T11:50:09.775594Z","last_update_date":"2022-06-06T11:50:09.778187Z","tool_type":["Desktop application"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:cnapy","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"klamt@mpi-magdeburg.mpg.de","name":"Steffen Klamt","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2563-7561","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Metabolic network modelling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3660"},{"term":"Network visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3925"}]}],"name":"CNApy","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Chan019","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/BIOINFORMATICS/BTAB828","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2021 The Author(s) 2021. Published by Oxford University Press.Summary: Constraint-based reconstruction and analysis (COBRA) is a widely used modeling framework for analyzing and designing metabolic networks. Here, we present CNApy, an open-source cross-platform desktop application written in Python, which offers a state-of-the-art graphical front-end for the intuitive analysis of metabolic networks with COBRA methods. While the basic look-and-feel of CNApy is similar to the user interface of the MATLAB toolbox CellNetAnalyzer, it provides various enhanced features by using components of the powerful Qt library. CNApy supports a number of standard and advanced COBRA techniques and further functionalities can be easily embedded in its GUI facilitating modular extension in the future.","authors":[{"name":"Bekiaris P.S."},{"name":"Klamt S."},{"name":"Schneider P."},{"name":"Thiele S."},{"name":"Von Kamp A."}],"date":"2022-03-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"CNApy: A C